{"maxId":0,"entries":{"copy|Another study found that only 11% of all residents in 19 nursing homes had":["u268"],"copy|received eye examinations in the last 2 years.":["u269"],"copy|TABLE 33-4 ■ CLINICAL LOW-VISION EXAMINATION":["u269"],"copy|Providing information about vision and vision impairment to the nursing home staff is important for assisting residents in using their remaining vision effectively.":["u270"],"copy|Magnification may also be provided by stand or handheld magnifiers, which are often more familiar to older adults who may have used them previously for maps":["u378"],"copy|or coins.":["u379"],"copy|TABLE 33-6 ■ LOW-VISION AND SPEECH-OUTPUT DEVICES":["u379"],"copy|These devices do not require a close eye-to-lens distance, but the closer to the eye the lens is held, the wider the field of view.":["u380"],"copy|For example, the glare from a sunny window onto a waxed":["u444"],"copy|floor, tabletops, and glass could cause objects in the dining room of a senior community to be obscured.":["u445"],"copy|TABLE 33-7 ■ WAYS TO MAKE AN ENVIRONMENT MORE VISUALLY ACCESSIBLE":["u445"],"copy|An older person with low vision might function as if blind in that environment and be unable to find a chair, recognize his friends, serve his plate, or identify the food in a buffet line.":["u446"],"copy|There are two schools":["u511"],"copy|of thought on the timing of rehabilitation related to adaptation.":["u513"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[23,664],[24,-1],[25,663],[26,198],[27,202],[28,203],[29,204],[30,205],[31,206],[32,-8],[35,215],[36,216],[37,217],[40,-13],[51,275],[66,-28],[70,619],[72,347],[73,-31],[77,377],[78,380],[79,386],[80,388],[81,-35],[94,484],[95,485],[97,486],[99,488],[101,489],[102,490],[104,492],[105,493],[107,494],[108,495],[109,-50],[273,-35],[285,-27],[295,-24],[350,-9],[387,-6],[425,-5],[449,-2],[456,14],[497,15],[498,16],[561,43]],"preservedBreaks":{"Another study found that only 11% of all residents in 19 nursing homes had received eye examinations in the last 2 years.":[74],"TABLE 33-4 ■ CLINICAL LOW-VISION EXAMINATION Providing information about vision and vision impairment to the nursing home staff is important for assisting residents in using their remaining vision effectively.":[44],"Magnification may also be provided by stand or handheld magnifiers, which are often more familiar to older adults who may have used them previously for maps or coins.":[156],"TABLE 33-6 ■ LOW-VISION AND SPEECH-OUTPUT DEVICES These devices do not require a close eye-to-lens distance, but the closer to the eye the lens is held, the wider the field of view.":[49],"For example, the glare from a sunny window onto a waxed floor, tabletops, and glass could cause objects in the dining room of a senior community to be obscured.":[55],"TABLE 33-7 ■ WAYS TO MAKE AN ENVIRONMENT MORE VISUALLY ACCESSIBLE An older person with low vision might function as if blind in that environment and be unable to find a chair, recognize his friends, serve his plate, or identify the food in a buffet line.":[65],"There are two schools of thought on the timing of rehabilitation related to adaptation.":[21]}}
